---
import Guide from "@layouts/Guide.astro";
import Alert from "@components/Alert.astro";
import { ABOUT_NAV } from "@data/nav-sections";
import { SITE } from "@data/site";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", href: "/" },
  { name: "About", href: "/about" },
  { name: "What is identity.org.au", href: "/about/what-is-identity-org-au" },
];
---

<Guide
  title="What is identity.org.au"
  metaTitle="What is identity.org.au — About | identity.org.au"
  description="identity.org.au is an open-source community identity service operated by a not-for-profit foundation. It is independent of the Australian Government."
  lede="An open-source community service for privacy-preserving digital identity, operated independently of government."
  eyebrow="About"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  sectionLabel="In this section"
  sectionNav={ABOUT_NAV}
>
  <Alert tone="warning" title="Not an Australian Government service">
    <p>
      identity.org.au is not operated or endorsed by the Australian Government or any state or
      territory government. It is separate from the Australian Government Digital ID System
      (AGDIS), myID and myGov. If you are looking for government digital identity, visit the
      government's own websites.
    </p>
    <p>
      DET-IO Pty Ltd, a predecessor company, received NSW Government business-grant funding.
      This does not involve government operation, control or endorsement of identity.org.au.
    </p>
    <p>
      The foundation is currently in the process of applying for accreditation under the
      <em>Digital ID Act 2024</em> Accreditation Scheme, administered by the Digital ID
      Regulator (the ACCC). Accreditation has not been granted, no accreditation is claimed,
      and the Digital ID Accreditation Trustmark will only be displayed if and when
      accreditation is granted and a Trade Mark Licence Agreement is in place. Progress will
      be reported on this page.
    </p>
  </Alert>

  <div class="prose-service mt-8">
    <h2>What this service is</h2>
    <p>
      identity.org.au is two things at once, deliberately. It is an <strong>advocacy and
      education hub</strong> for privacy-preserving digital identity — publishing plain-English
      guidance, policy analysis and <a href="/insights">evergreen research explainers</a> about
      how identity systems should treat people. It is also the public home of the planned
      <a href="/wallet">Identity Wallet</a>. The wallet and its web portal are planned for the
      January 2027 launch window; <a href="/launch">join the launch list</a> for updates.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two roles reinforce each other and share one constraint. The analysis we publish holds
      the service we operate to account — every principle argued in
      <a href="/insights">Insights</a> is implemented, checkably, in the wallet. The wallet itself
      is <strong>open-source reference software</strong>, powered by a technology layer called
      VEID (Verifiable Electronic Identity): a complete, working implementation that anyone can inspect, run and build on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both roles sit with one foundation. identity.org.au is operated by
      {SITE.foundation}, an Australian not-for-profit public company limited by guarantee
      (ACN {SITE.acn}), whose constitution places the technology under a public-benefit lock: no
      dividends, no private capture of the IP, and winding-up provisions that pass everything to
      another public-benefit entity. These commitments apply to the website and the wallet
      reference implementation; it does <strong>not</strong> make the Foundation the operator of
      VEID. VEID is decentralised protocol technology run by participating validators, which
      anyone may join subject to the network's rules. The education mission and the service are bound by the same
      document — see <a href="/about/who-runs-it">who runs it</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How it differs from government digital ID</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="table-gov">
      <caption class="sr-only">Comparison between this service and government digital identity</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col"></th>
          <th scope="col">identity.org.au</th>
          <th scope="col">Government digital ID</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Operated by</th>
          <td>A not-for-profit foundation, under a public constitution</td>
          <td>Government agencies, under legislation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Code</th>
          <td>Fully open source — anyone can inspect it</td>
          <td>Generally not public</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Accreditation</th>
          <td>None claimed. The design aligns with the principles of data minimisation and consent-based sharing, but alignment is not accreditation</td>
          <td>Operates within a legislated accreditation scheme</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Where your identity lives</th>
          <td>Encrypted, anchored to a decentralised network — no single central database</td>
          <td>Government-operated systems</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Who can use verification results</th>
          <td>Services on the VirtEngine network, with your per-request consent</td>
          <td>Accredited relying parties</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-service mt-8">
    <h2>Why we lead with this</h2>
    <p>
      Trust in an identity system starts with knowing exactly who runs it. Blurring the line
      between community infrastructure and government services would be a form of deception — so
      every page of this site carries the distinction, and we make no claims of government
      status, endorsement or accreditation anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we do claim, you can verify: the <a href="/about/open-source">code is public</a>, the
      <a href="/about/who-runs-it">foundation's constitution</a> is a public document, and the
      <a href="/about/patents">patent</a> is on the public register.
    </p>
  </div>
</Guide>
